afort cooperate entirely not only  into the actual crimes that he was  indicted and  convicted of but also for any  investigations that mueller wants his  cooperation  with and that manafort might have some  information that would help out  manafort pleaded pursuant to an  agreement that required his fully  truthfully completely and forthrightly  cooperating  with the government according to the  sentencing memo that the mueller team  just filed the plea agreement provides  that if the defendant  manafort fails to fulfill completely  each and every one of his obligations  under the plea agreement or quote  engages in any criminal activity prior  to sentencing  the defendant manafort will be in breach  of the agreement  and of course a breach relieves the  government of any obligations under the  plea agreement  including its agreement to a reduction  of the sentencing guidelines  for acceptance and responsibility but  leaves intact all of the obligations of  the defendant as well  as his guilty pleas which means that if  manafort violated the plea agreement  he is not entitled to any reduction of  any of the crimes that he was convicted  of  and in fact he cannot withdraw his  guilty plea as a result  no matter what manafort is going to be  stuck with his admission of guilt  with no reduction in sentence in a  status report that the mueller team  recently filed both in dc and virginia  according to mueller after signing the  plea agreement manafort committed  federal crimes by lying to the federal  bureau of investigation  and the special counsel's office on a  variety of subject matters  which constitute breaches of the  agreement the government will file a  detailed sentencing submission to the  probation department  and the court in advance of the  sentencing that sets forth the nature of  the defendant's crimes and lies  including those after signing the plea  agreement hear it  this is a bombshell filing by the  mueller team  it is very rare for these kinds of plea  agreements to blow up  and what the mueller team has hinted at  here is that manafort not only just lied  once  and failed to communicate but lied on a  variety of different  subjects so what are the consequences of  this plea agreement blowing up  well for one the mueller team won't be  able to use paul manafort as a  cooperating witness they won't be able  to rely on his testimony  at trial they won't get him to waive his  fifth amendment rights against  self-incrimination  and of course paul manafort is going to  jail for a very very long time  the reason you enter into one of these  plea agreements is to get a reduction in  your sentence you make it easier for the  government to prove their case you admit  your guilt and in exchange the  government gives you  a letter saying that you should get a  reduction in sentence a judge has some  leeway in what sentence is actually  handed down  there are federal guidelines federal  sentencing guidelines that provide a  certain range  and the judge is allowed to choose from  within that range and the government  states that because you cooperated with  their investigation you should get a  reduction in your sentence at the low  end of the sentencing guideline range  of course when you back out of a plea  agreement like this  the government no longer needs to say  nice things about you and no longer  needs to argue that you should get a  reduction in your sentence and in fact  they will do exactly the opposite  instead the government will argue that  because of your bad conduct  you should get an enhancement in your  sentence you should be at the high end  of the range or  if you engaged in new crimes then you  can be sentenced to  additional time that was not reflected  in your original guilty plea  so paul manafort is in a world of hurt  manafort's breach of the plea agreement  and lies to federal prosecutors may be  related to a report coming out of the  uk's guardian newspaper  which reports that paul manafort met  extensively with julian assange of  wikileaks  before the 2016 election if that is  proved to be true that could have huge  implications for the conspiracy and  collusion investigation that's going on  on top of that there is yet another  bombshell that reports that while  manafort was acting  as a cooperating witness with the  mueller team both manafort  and his attorneys were passing secret  information  to president trump and the other trump  campaign defendants  this is a huge bombshell  generally speaking if you talk to your  lawyer those communications are  protected by  attorney-client privilege no surprise  there if you talk to  a third party who is not your attorney  the general rule is  there is no attorney-client privilege  whatsoever those communications are not  privileged and they are discoverable  however when you have multiple  defendants on one side  often they share what's called a common  interest which means that  what's good for one defendant is also  good for the other defendants and as a  result  it's very favorable to be able to share  information  among different defendants and their  lawyers and you wouldn't want to share  that information if it breached  attorney-client privilege and those  communications  were exposed so the court recognizes  what's called a  common interest doctrine which allows  those defendants to communicate back and  forth  to do that you have to sign what's  called a joint defense agreement which  basically just codifies that  relationship  and allows communications to go back and  forth however the wrinkle comes  when one of those defendants no longer  shares a common interest with the other  defendants  when one of those defendants enters into  a plea agreement  that defendant's interests are no longer  aligned with the other defendants the  other defendants presumably want to  fight the case and want to be proven  innocent whereas the defendant who has  pled guilty  wants a reduction in sentence and wants  to 